#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:263
msgid "SMMU configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:265
msgid "Machine-wide SMMUv3 IOMMU"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:266
msgid ""
"Setting the machine-specific option ``iommu=smmuv3`` causes QEMU to create a "
"single, machine-wide SMMUv3 instance that applies to all devices in the PCIe "
"topology."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:270
msgid ""
"For information about selectively bypassing devices, refer to ``docs/bypass-"
"iommu.txt``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:273
msgid "User-creatable SMMUv3 devices"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:274
msgid ""
"You can use the ``-device arm-smmuv3`` option to create multiple user-"
"defined SMMUv3 devices, each associated with a separate PCIe root complex. "
"This is only permitted if the machine-wide SMMUv3 (``iommu=smmuv3``) option "
"is not used. Each ``arm-smmuv3`` device uses the ``primary-bus`` sub-option "
"to specify which PCIe root complex it is associated with."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:281
msgid ""
"This model is useful when you want to mirror a host configuration where each "
"NUMA node typically has its own SMMU, allowing the VM topology to align more "
"closely with the host’s hardware layout."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:285 ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:305
msgid "Example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:287
msgid ""
"-device arm-smmuv3,primary-bus=pcie.0,id=smmuv3.0\n"
"...\n"
"-device pxb-pcie,id=pcie.1,numa_node=1\n"
"-device arm-smmuv3,primary-bus=pcie.1,id=smmuv3.1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:292
msgid "*Accelerated SMMUv3 (nested translation)*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:294
msgid ""
"The ``accel=on`` option enables hardware-accelerated nested translation for "
"vfio-pci passthrough devices. In this mode the guest SMMU driver programs "
"its own Stage-1 page tables, with the host SMMUv3 handling both Stage-1 "
"(guest) and Stage-2 (host) translations in hardware. The host SMMUv3 must "
"support nested translation. This mode requires the iommufd backend and is "
"only supported when booting with ACPI (not device tree)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:301
msgid ""
"When ``accel=on``, QEMU automatically derives the values for the ``ril``, "
"``ats``, ``oas``, ``ssidsize`` and ``cmdqv`` sub-options from the host "
"SMMUv3 capabilities unless they are set explicitly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:307
msgid "-device arm-smmuv3,primary-bus=pcie.0,id=smmuv3.0,accel=on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:309
msgid "*Accelerated SMMUv3 command queues (Tegra241 CMDQV)*"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:311
msgid ""
"The ``cmdqv`` sub-option enables NVIDIA Tegra241 Command Queue "
"Virtualization (CMDQV) on supported hosts. With CMDQV, each accelerated "
"``arm-smmuv3`` device gets dedicated hardware command queues and the guest "
"issues SMMU invalidation commands directly to real hardware, bypassing QEMU "
"and improving throughput for workloads that issue many invalidations. "
"Without it, every invalidation command traps into QEMU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:319
msgid "SBSA Generic Watchdog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:321
msgid ""
"The SBSA Generic Watchdog Timer (GWDT) can be added to the virt machine "
"using ``-device sbsa-gwdt``. It is only supported on the virt machine, which "
"wires up statically assigned MMIO regions and IRQs via machine-specific plug "
"handlers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:326
msgid "Two modes are available:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:328
msgid "Native mode (default)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:329
msgid ""
"The watchdog is described via the ACPI GTDT table and FDT, using the system "
"counter frequency. Example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:332
msgid "-device sbsa-gwdt"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:334
msgid "WDAT mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:335
msgid ""
"The watchdog is described via the ACPI WDAT table (no FDT node), using a 1 "
"kHz timer frequency. WDAT and GTDT watchdog entries are mutually exclusive. "
"Example::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:339
msgid "-device sbsa-gwdt,wdat=on"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:342
msgid "Linux guest kernel configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:344
msgid ""
"The 'defconfig' for Linux arm and arm64 kernels should include the right "
"device drivers for virtio and the PCI controller; however some older kernel "
"versions, especially for 32-bit Arm, did not have everything enabled by "
"default. If you're not seeing PCI devices that you expect, then check that "
"your guest config has::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:350
msgid ""
"CONFIG_PCI=y\n"
"CONFIG_VIRTIO_PCI=y\n"
"CONFIG_PCI_HOST_GENERIC=y"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:354
msgid ""
"If you want to use the ``virtio-gpu-pci`` graphics device you will also "
"need::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:357
msgid ""
"CONFIG_DRM=y\n"
"CONFIG_DRM_VIRTIO_GPU=y"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:361
msgid "Hardware configuration information for bare-metal programming"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:363
msgid ""
"The ``virt`` board automatically generates a device tree blob (\"dtb\") "
"which it passes to the guest. This provides information about the addresses, "
"interrupt lines and other configuration of the various devices in the "
"system. Guest code can rely on and hard-code the following addresses:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:369
msgid "Flash memory starts at address 0x0000_0000"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:371
msgid "RAM starts at 0x4000_0000"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:373
msgid ""
"All other information about device locations may change between QEMU "
"versions, so guest code must look in the DTB."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:376
msgid ""
"QEMU supports two types of guest image boot for ``virt``, and the way for "
"the guest code to locate the dtb binary differs:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:379
msgid ""
"For guests using the Linux kernel boot protocol (this means any non-ELF file "
"passed to the QEMU ``-kernel`` option) the address of the DTB is passed in a "
"register (``r2`` for 32-bit guests, or ``x0`` for 64-bit guests)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../system/arm/virt.rst:384
msgid ""
"For guests booting as \"bare-metal\" (any other kind of boot), the DTB is at "
"the start of RAM (0x4000_0000)"
msgstr ""
